# Dependencies

Scripting define symbols are set in `ProjectSettings.asset` (`scriptingDefineSymbols`). The core set below is applied to the project; `VIDE` and `SALSA` are **documented only** and are not set in the project.

## All projects (core)

These are the baseline — virtually every stoogebag assembly assumes them.

| Area | Required | Scripting define |
|---|---|---|
| Tweening | **DOTween** + **DOTween Modules** (free) / DOTween Pro | `DOTWEEN` |
| Async | **UniTask** | `UNITASK` |
| Reactive | **UniRx** | `UNIRX` |
| Async + tweening | UniTask + DOTween integration | `UNITASK_DOTWEEN_SUPPORT` |
| UI text | **TextMeshPro** (com.unity.ugui) | — |
| Input | **Input System** | — |
| Rendering | **URP** | — |
| Camera | **Cinemachine** | `CINEMACHINE` (v2) / `NEW_CINEMACHINE` (v3) |
| Sequencing | **Timeline** | `TIMELINE` |
| Assets | **Addressables** | — |
| Dialogue text | **Febucci Text Animator** | `TEXT_ANIMATOR` |
| Dialogue | **VIDE** (for dialogues) | `VIDE` |

Core define symbols (docs reference):

```
DOTWEEN
UNITASK
UNIRX
UNITASK_DOTWEEN_SUPPORT
CINEMACHINE
NEW_CINEMACHINE
TIMELINE
TEXT_ANIMATOR
VIDE
```

`CINEMACHINE` gates the `stoogebag.cinemachine` assembly (required); `NEW_CINEMACHINE` additionally selects the Cinemachine 3.x API inside it. This project uses Cinemachine 3.x, so both are set. `DOTWEEN` and `UNITASK_DOTWEEN_SUPPORT` may be auto-provided by their packages; setting them manually is harmless. `VIDE` is documented here but intentionally not set in the project.

## Tier 2

| Area | Required | Scripting define |
|---|---|---|
| Audio | **FMOD** | — |
| Lip sync | **SALSA** (+ RootMotion) | `SALSA` |

`SALSA` is documented here but intentionally not set in the project.

